## an4.16
an4.16:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four kinds of subtlety. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four kinds of exquisiteness.
an4.16:1.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
an4.16:1.3 | S: A mendicant has ultimate subtlety of form. | T: (1) Here, a bhikkhu possesses supreme exquisiteness of form.
an4.16:1.4 | S: They don’t see any other subtlety of form that’s better or finer than that, | T: He does not perceive any other exquisiteness of form more excellent or sublime than that one; he does not yearn for any other exquisiteness of form more excellent or sublime than that one.
an4.16:1.5 | S: nor do they aim for it. | T: (none)
an4.16:1.6 | S: A mendicant has ultimate subtlety of feeling. | T: (2) He possesses supreme exquisiteness of feeling …
an4.16:1.7 | S: They don’t see any other subtlety of feeling that’s better or finer than that, | T: (none)
an4.16:1.8 | S: nor do they aim for it. | T: (none)
an4.16:1.9 | S: A mendicant has ultimate subtlety of perception. | T: (3) … supreme exquisiteness of perception …
an4.16:1.10 | S: They don’t see any other subtlety of perception that’s better or finer than that, | T: (none)
an4.16:1.11 | S: nor do they aim for it. | T: (none)
an4.16:1.12 | S: A mendicant has ultimate subtlety of choices. | T: (4) … supreme exquisiteness of volitional activities.
an4.16:1.13 | S: They don’t see any other subtlety of choices that’s better or finer than that, | T: He does not perceive any other exquisiteness of volitional activities more excellent or sublime than that one; he does not yearn for any other exquisiteness of volitional activities more excellent or sublime than that one.
an4.16:1.14 | S: nor do they aim for it. | T: (none)
an4.16:1.15 | S: These are the four kinds of subtlety. | T: “These are the four kinds of exquisiteness.”
an4.16:2.1 | S: Knowing the subtlety of form, | T: Having known the exquisiteness of form,
an4.16:2.2 | S: the cause of feelings, | T: the origination of feelings,
an4.16:2.3 | S: where perception comes from, | T: how perception arises,
