## sn47.14
sn47.14:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Vajjis near Ukkacelā on the bank of the Ganges River, together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants. It was not long after Sāriputta and Moggallāna had become fully quenched. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Vajjians at Ukkacela on the bank of the river Ganges, together with a great Bhikkhu Saṅgha, not long after Sāriputta and Moggallana had attained final Nibbāna.
sn47.14:1.2 | S: Now, at that time the Buddha was sitting in the open, surrounded by the Saṅgha of mendicants. | T: Now on that occasion the Blessed One was sitting in the open air in the midst of the Bhikkhu Saṅgha.
sn47.14:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha looked around the Saṅgha of mendicants, who were silent. He addressed them: | T: Then the Blessed One, having surveyed the silent Bhikkhu Saṅgha, addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn47.14:2.2 | S: “Mendicants, this assembly seems empty to me now that Sāriputta and Moggallāna have become fully quenched. | T: “Bhikkhus, this assembly appears to me empty now that Sāriputta and Moggallana have attained final Nibbāna.
sn47.14:2.3 | S: My assembly never used to be empty; I had no concern for any region where Sāriputta and Moggallāna stayed. | T: This assembly was not empty for me earlier, and I had no concern for whatever quarter Sāriputta and Moggallana were dwelling in.
sn47.14:2.4 | S: The Buddhas of the past or the future have pairs of chief disciples who at best were like | T: “The Arahants, the Perfectly Enlightened Ones, who arose in the past also had just such a supreme pair of disciples as I had in Sāriputta and Moggallana.
sn47.14:2.5 | S: Sāriputta and Moggallāna were to me. | T: (none)
sn47.14:2.6 | P: Yepi te, bhikkhave, bhavissanti anāgatamaddhānaṁ arahanto sammāsambuddhā, tesampi bhagavantānaṁ etapparamaṁyeva sāvakayugaṁ bhavissati—seyyathāpi mayhaṁ sāriputtamoggallānā. | T: The Arahants, the Perfectly Enlightened Ones, who will arise in the future will also have just such a supreme pair of disciples as I had in Sāriputta and Moggallana.
sn47.14:2.7 | S: It’s an incredible and amazing quality of such disciples | T: “It is wonderful, bhikkhus, on the part of the disciples, it is amazing on the part of the disciples,
sn47.14:2.8 | S: that they fulfill the Teacher’s instructions and follow his advice. And they’re liked and approved, respected and admired by the four assemblies. | T: that they will act in accordance with the Teacher’s instructions and comply with his admonitions, that they will be dear and agreeable to the four assemblies, that they will be revered and esteemed by them.
sn47.14:2.9 | S: And it’s an incredible and amazing quality of the Realized One | T: It is wonderful, bhikkhus, on the part of the Tathagata, it is amazing on the part of the Tathagata,
sn47.14:2.10 | S: that when such a pair of disciples becomes fully quenched he does not sorrow or lament. | T: that when such a pair of disciples has attained final Nibbāna, there is no sorrow or lamentation in the Tathagata.
sn47.14:2.11 | S: How could it possibly be so that what is born, created, conditioned, and liable to wear out should not wear out? | T: “How, bhikkhus, is it to be obtained here: ‘May what is born, come to be, conditioned, and subject to disintegration not disintegrate! ’?
sn47.14:2.12 | S: That is not possible. | T: That is impossible.
sn47.14:2.13 | S: Suppose there was a large tree standing with heartwood, and the largest branches broke off. | T: It is just as if the largest branches would break off a great tree standing possessed of heartwood:
sn47.14:2.14 | S: In the same way, in the great Saṅgha that stands with heartwood, Sāriputta and Moggallāna have become fully quenched. | T: so too, bhikkhus, in the great Bhikkhu Saṅgha standing possessed of heartwood, Sāriputta and Moggallana have attained final Nibbāna.
sn47.14:2.15 | S: How could it possibly be so that what is born, created, conditioned, and liable to wear out should not wear out? | T: How, bhikkhus, is it to be obtained here: ‘May what is born, come to be, conditioned, and subject to disintegration not disintegrate!’?
sn47.14:2.16 | S: That is not possible. | T: That is impossible.
sn47.14:2.17 | S: So mendicants, live as your own island, your own refuge, with no other refuge. Let the teaching be your island and your refuge, with no other refuge. | T: “Therefore, bhikkhus, dwell with yourselves as your own island, with yourselves as your own refuge, with no other refuge; dwell with the Dhamma as your island, with the Dhamma as your refuge, with no other refuge
sn47.14:3.1 | S: And how does a mendicant do this? | T: (none)
sn47.14:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (none)
sn47.14:3.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: (none)
sn47.14:3.4 | S: mind … | T: (none)
sn47.14:3.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (none)
sn47.14:3.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant lives with themselves as island and refuge, without other refuge; with the teachings as island and refuge, without other refuge. | T: … as in §9 …
sn47.14:3.7 | S: Whether now or after I have passed, any who shall live as their own island, their own refuge, with no other refuge; with the teaching as their island and their refuge, with no other refuge—those mendicants of mine eager to train shall be among the best of the best.” | T: Those bhikkhus, either now or after I am gone, who dwell with themselves as their own island, with themselves as their own refuge, with no other refuge; with the Dhamma as their island, with the Dhamma as their refuge, with no other refuge—it is these bhikkhus who will be for me topmost of those keen on the training.”
sn47.14:3.8 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.81
sn22.81:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Kosambi in Ghosita’s Park.
sn22.81:1.2 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Kosambī for alms. | T: Then, in the morning, the Blessed One dressed and, taking bowl and robe, entered Kosambi for alms.
sn22.81:1.3 | S: After the meal, on his return from almsround, he set his lodgings in order himself. Taking his bowl and robe, without informing his supporters or taking leave of the mendicant Saṅgha, he set out to go wandering alone, with no companion. | T: When he had walked for alms in Kosambi and had returned from the alms round, after his meal he set his lodging in order himself, took his bowl and robe, and without informing his personal attendants, without taking leave of the Bhikkhu Saṅgha, he set out on tour alone, without a companion.
sn22.81:2.1 | S: Then, not long after the Buddha had left, one of the mendicants went to Venerable Ānanda and told him what had happened. | T: Then, not long after the Blessed One had departed, a certain bhikkhu approached the Venerable Ānanda and told him:
sn22.81:2.2 | P: “esāvuso ānanda, bhagavā sāmaṁ senāsanaṁ saṁsāmetvā pattacīvaramādāya anāmantetvā upaṭṭhāke anapaloketvā bhikkhusaṅghaṁ eko adutiyo cārikaṁ pakkanto”ti. | T: “Friend Ānanda, the Blessed One has set his lodging in order himself, taken his bowl and robe, and without informing his personal attendants, without taking leave of the Bhikkhu Saṅgha, he has set out on tour alone, without a companion.”
sn22.81:2.3 | S: Ānanda said, “Reverend, when the Buddha leaves like this it means he wants to stay alone. | T: “Friend, whenever the Blessed One sets out like that he wishes to dwell alone.
sn22.81:2.4 | S: At this time no one should follow him.” | T: On such an occasion the Blessed One should not be followed by anyone.”
sn22.81:3.1 | S: Then the Buddha, traveling stage by stage, arrived at Pārileyyaka, | T: Then the Blessed One, wandering by stages, arrived at Parileyyaka.
sn22.81:3.2 | S: where he stayed at the root of a sacred sal tree. | T: There at Parileyyaka the Blessed One dwelt at the foot of an auspicious sal tree.
sn22.81:3.3 | S: Then several mendicants went up to Venerable Ānanda and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then a number of bhikkhus approached the Venerable Ānanda and exchanged greetings with him.
sn22.81:3.4 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side and said to him, | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, they sat down to one side and said to the Venerable Ānanda:
sn22.81:3.5 | S: “Reverend, it’s been a long time since we’ve heard a Dhamma talk from the Buddha. | T: “Friend Ānanda, it has been a long time since we heard a Dhamma talk in the presence of the Blessed One.
sn22.81:3.6 | S: We wish to hear a Dhamma talk from the Buddha.” | T: We should like to hear such a talk, friend Ānanda.”
sn22.81:4.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda together with those mendicants went to Pārileyyaka to see the Buddha. They bowed and sat down to one side, | T: Then the Venerable Ānanda together with those bhikkhus approached the Blessed One at Parileyyaka, at the foot of the auspicious sal tree. Having approached, they paid homage to the Blessed One and sat down to one side.
sn22.81:4.2 | S: and the Buddha educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired them with a Dhamma talk. | T: The Blessed One then instructed, exhorted, inspired, and gladdened those bhikkhus with a Dhamma talk.
sn22.81:4.3 | S: Now at that time one of the monks had the thought, | T: Now on that occasion a reflection arose in the mind of a certain bhikkhu thus:
sn22.81:4.4 | S: “How do you know and see in order to end the defilements without delay?” | T: “How should one know, how should one see, for the immediate destruction of the taints to occur?”
sn22.81:4.5 | S: Then the Buddha, knowing that monk’s train of thought, addressed the mendicants: | T: The Blessed One, having known with his own mind the reflection in that bhikkhu’s mind, addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn22.81:4.6 | S: “Mendicants, I’ve taught the Dhamma analytically. | T: “Bhikkhus, this Dhamma has been taught by me discriminately.
sn22.81:4.7 | S: I’ve analytically taught the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, | T: The four establishments of mindfulness have been taught by me discriminately.
sn22.81:4.8 | S: the four right efforts, | T: The four right strivings …
sn22.81:4.9 | S: the four bases of psychic power, | T: The four bases for spiritual power …
sn22.81:4.10 | S: the five faculties, | T: The five spiritual faculties …
sn22.81:4.11 | S: the five powers, | T: The five powers …
sn22.81:4.12 | S: the seven awakening factors, | T: The seven factors of enlightenment …
sn22.81:4.13 | S: and the noble eightfold path. | T: The Noble Eightfold Path has been taught by me discriminately.
sn22.81:4.14 | S: That’s how I’ve taught the Dhamma analytically. | T: (none)
sn22.81:4.15 | S: Though I’ve taught the Dhamma analytically, still a certain mendicant present here has this thought: | T: Bhikkhus, in regard to the Dhamma that has been thus taught by me discriminately, a reflection arose in the mind of a certain bhikkhu thus:
sn22.81:4.16 | S: ‘How do you know and see in order to end the defilements without delay?’ | T: ‘How should one know, how should one see, for the immediate destruction of the taints to occur?’
sn22.81:5.1 | S: And how, mendicants, do you know and see in order to end the defilements without delay? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, should one know, how should one see, for the immediate destruction of the taints to occur?
sn22.81:5.2 | S: Take an unlearned ordinary person who has not seen the noble ones, and is neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve not seen true persons, and are neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the true persons. | T: Here, bhikkhus, the uninstructed worldling, who is not a seer of the noble ones and is unskilled and undisciplined in their Dhamma, who is not a seer of superior persons and is unskilled and undisciplined in their Dhamma,
sn22.81:5.3 | S: They regard form as self. | T: regards form as self.
sn22.81:5.4 | S: But that regarding is just a conditioned phenomenon. | T: That regarding, bhikkhus, is a formation.
sn22.81:5.5 | S: And what’s the source, origin, birthplace, and inception of that conditioned phenomenon? | T: That formation—what is its source, what is its origin, from what is it born and produced?
sn22.81:5.6 | S: When an unlearned ordinary person is struck by feelings born of contact with ignorance, craving arises. | T: When the uninstructed worldling is contacted by a feeling born of ignorance-contact, craving arises:
sn22.81:5.7 | S: That conditioned phenomenon is born from that. | T: thence that formation is born.
sn22.81:5.8 | S: So that conditioned phenomenon is impermanent, conditioned, and dependently originated. | T: “Thus, bhikkhus, that formation is impermanent, conditioned, dependently arisen;
